  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is sericulture? (3 marks)', 'Sericulture is the rearing of silkworms to produce raw silk. The common mulberry silkworm, Bombyx mori, is fed on mulberry leaves. Its life cycle is egg → larva (caterpillar) → pupa inside a cocoon → adult moth. The larva spins a cocoon of a continuous silk thread made of the protein fibroin. Cocoons are collected, boiled or steamed to kill the pupa, and the thread is unwound (reeled) and spun into silk yarn.
Other types are eri silk (worms fed on castor leaves), muga and tasar. In Bhutan, eri and mulberry silk are produced in some dzongkhags and used in traditional weaving of kira and gho, giving rural households extra income.', null, '[{"point": "Sericulture: rearing of silkworms for the production of raw silk", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Silkworm (Bombyx mori) fed on mulberry leaves; life cycle egg → larva → pupa in cocoon → moth; cocoons boiled/reeled to obtain silk thread", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Other types (eri, muga, tasar); in Bhutan eri silk (castor) and mulberry silk are reared, supporting traditional weaving (kira, gho) and rural income", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
