  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Define immunity. Name its types. (3 marks)', 'Immunity is the ability of the body to resist disease by recognising and destroying pathogens (bacteria, viruses, parasites) and their toxins.
Types:
1. Innate (natural, non-specific) immunity - present from birth and acts against all invaders: barriers such as skin and mucous membranes, stomach acid and lysozyme, phagocytic cells (neutrophils, macrophages), fever and inflammation.
2. Acquired (adaptive, specific) immunity - develops after exposure to a particular antigen and has memory, through B lymphocytes (antibodies) and T lymphocytes. It may be active (the body makes its own antibodies after infection or vaccination - long-lasting) or passive (ready-made antibodies received from the mother or as antiserum/immunoglobulin - immediate but short-lived).', null, '[{"point": "Immunity: the ability of the body to resist/defend against infection by pathogens and their toxins", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Innate (natural, non-specific) immunity: skin, mucous membranes, phagocytes, fever, inflammation - present from birth", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Acquired (adaptive, specific) immunity: active (infection, vaccination) and passive (maternal antibodies, antiserum); involves B and T lymphocytes and memory", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
