  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Define osmotic pressure. (3 marks)', 'Osmotic pressure is the pressure that must be applied to a solution to prevent the flow of pure solvent into it through a semipermeable membrane - in other words, the pressure needed to stop osmosis.
It is a colligative property: it depends on the number of solute particles, not their nature, and is given by van ''t Hoff''s equation π = CRT (C = molar concentration, R = gas constant, T = absolute temperature). A more concentrated solution has a higher osmotic pressure.
In the body, osmotic pressure controls the movement of water between cells and their surroundings; the osmotic (oncotic) pressure of plasma proteins keeps fluid in the blood vessels, and intravenous fluids such as 0.9% saline are made isotonic with blood so that red cells neither swell nor shrink.', null, '[{"point": "Osmotic pressure: the minimum pressure that must be applied to a solution to stop the flow of pure solvent into it through a semipermeable membrane (prevent osmosis)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Depends on the number of solute particles (colligative): π = CRT (van ''t Hoff); higher concentration → higher osmotic pressure", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Importance: maintains water balance of cells; plasma proteins (oncotic pressure) hold fluid in blood vessels; isotonic IV fluids like 0.9% saline", "marks": 0.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
