  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Define turgor pressure and state its importance in plants. (3 marks)', 'Turgor pressure is the pressure exerted by the protoplast (mainly the vacuole) against the cell wall when a plant cell has absorbed water by osmosis. The cell wall pushes back with an equal and opposite force, called wall pressure. A cell in this state is turgid.
Importance: turgor keeps cells firm, so non-woody parts such as leaves, flowers and young stems stay upright; when water is lost, turgor falls and the plant wilts. Turgor pressure also provides the force for cell enlargement during growth and controls the opening and closing of stomata through changes in the guard cells.', null, '[{"point": "Turgor pressure: the pressure exerted by the cell contents (vacuole) against the cell wall when the cell has taken in water by osmosis (equal and opposite to wall pressure)", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Importance: keeps cells turgid and non-woody plant parts (leaves, young stems) firm and upright; wilting when it falls", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Also drives cell enlargement/growth and the opening and closing of stomata (guard cells)", "marks": 0.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
