  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is the role of haemoglobin in respiration? (3 marks)', 'Haemoglobin is the red, iron-containing protein pigment in red blood cells. Each molecule has four haem groups, each with an iron (Fe²⁺) atom that can bind one oxygen molecule.
In the lungs, where the partial pressure of oxygen is high, haemoglobin combines with oxygen to form oxyhaemoglobin, so blood carries about 70 times more oxygen than plasma alone could. In the tissues, where oxygen pressure is low and carbon dioxide and acidity are high (Bohr effect), oxyhaemoglobin releases its oxygen for cellular respiration.
Haemoglobin also carries part of the carbon dioxide (about 20-25%) back to the lungs as carbaminohaemoglobin and buffers H⁺ ions, helping transport of CO₂ as bicarbonate.', null, '[{"point": "Haemoglobin is the iron-containing red pigment of red blood cells; each molecule has 4 haem groups and can bind 4 O₂", "marks": 1}, {"point": "In the lungs (high pO₂) it combines with oxygen to form oxyhaemoglobin; in tissues (low pO₂, high CO₂, low pH) it releases oxygen", "marks": 1}, {"point": "It also carries some CO₂ (as carbaminohaemoglobin, about 20-25%) and buffers H⁺, helping CO₂ transport back to the lungs", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
