  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'What is spermatogenesis? (3 marks)', 'Spermatogenesis is the process by which male gametes (sperm) are produced in the seminiferous tubules of the testes, continuously from puberty.
Stages: (1) multiplication - diploid spermatogonia divide by mitosis; (2) growth - some enlarge into primary spermatocytes (2n); (3) maturation - each primary spermatocyte undergoes meiosis I to form two haploid secondary spermatocytes, and meiosis II gives four haploid spermatids; (4) spermiogenesis - spermatids change into motile spermatozoa with a head (nucleus and acrosome), middle piece (mitochondria) and tail.
The process takes about 64-74 days and is controlled by FSH and testosterone (released under LH); Sertoli cells nourish the developing sperm.', null, '[{"point": "Spermatogenesis: the formation of sperm (spermatozoa) from germ cells in the seminiferous tubules of the testes, from puberty onwards", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Stages: spermatogonia (2n) multiply by mitosis → primary spermatocytes → meiosis I → secondary spermatocytes (n) → meiosis II → spermatids (n) → spermiogenesis → sperm; one spermatocyte gives 4 sperm", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "Controlled by FSH and testosterone (LH); Sertoli cells nourish developing sperm", "marks": 0.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
